# DataHub frontend customization

## Scope and source of truth
- Applies to the `datahub-web-react/` directory of our pinned DataHub OSS fork.
- Before changes, inspect the actual components, state-management patterns, routing, GraphQL operations, build commands, and tests in this DataHub revision. Do not assume the upstream frontend architecture is unchanged between releases.
- Follow the existing repository's conventions and tooling (TypeScript, React, styling, formatting, testing) before introducing new libraries or patterns.
- Check `marketplace-platform/docs/architecture.md` for cross-service boundaries when accessible; do not assume cross-root rules automatically apply to this repository.

## Preserve upstream compatibility
- Prefer adding isolated marketplace routes, components, and adapters over rewriting DataHub core screens or shared packages.
- Reuse existing DataHub UI primitives, GraphQL client, routing, authorization controls, and session handling where they fit.
- Do not edit `metadata-service/`, `datahub-graphql-core/`, or generated GraphQL/API code as part of a UI task without an explicit, approved cross-repository change.
- Do not change upstream authentication flows, token management, GraphQL schemas, or metadata semantics just to simplify a frontend feature.
- Document unavoidable upstream-file modifications and why extension or composition was insufficient. Keep diffs small to ease future merges.

## Marketplace UI behavior
- Implement product discovery, search, filters, details, ownership, domains, and asset relationships using DataHub metadata and existing APIs where available.
- Separate marketplace business workflows (access requests, approvals, subscriptions) from catalog metadata. Use the marketplace service API for workflow actions, not invented DataHub GraphQL mutations.
- Introduce a typed service/adapter boundary around marketplace API calls. Keep network logic out of presentational components.
- Support loading, empty, error, unauthorized, and partial-data states. Do not silently treat failed access checks as empty results.
- Do not present mock product data as if it came from a connected DataHub instance; mark demos and fixtures explicitly.

## Security and permissions
- Preserve the existing OIDC/session model and route requests through the established authenticated server/backend boundary.
- Never place personal access tokens, client secrets, service credentials, or privileged GMS calls in browser code.
- Do not rely on hidden buttons or frontend-only filtering as authorization. Marketplace API and/or GMS must enforce relevant permissions server-side.
- DataHub Core search results may require additional authorization-aware filtering; do not assume that discovery results are permission-filtered for sensitive metadata.
- Encode and validate user-controlled input; avoid unsafe HTML and leaking sensitive values in logs or analytics.

## UI quality and tests
- Use accessible labels, semantic controls, keyboard navigation, focus states, and readable error feedback.
- Keep new components focused; prefer composition and existing patterns over monolithic pages.
- Add tests for modified behavior, including loading, errors, missing metadata, unauthorized states, and key user journeys.
- Run the smallest applicable repository lint/typecheck/test command and report what was actually executed.
- For visual changes, verify desktop and narrow layouts; do not claim visual verification without checking rendered output.

## Delivery expectations
- Before implementation: identify the touched files and any DataHub version/API assumptions.
- After implementation: summarize source changes, compatibility concerns, tests run, and any unverified behavior.
